  '"Wide skillet","Finely grated microplane","Tongs"',
  '[{"name":"Fresh Tonnarelli or thick spaghetti","amount":250,"unit":"g","notes":"Bronze-cut, starch-rich","category":"Pantry"},{"name":"Pecorino Romano D.O.P., finely grated like snow","amount":150,"unit":"g","notes":"Sharp sheep milk cheese","category":"Dairy"},{"name":"Whole black peppercorns","amount":1.5,"unit":"tbsp","notes":"Toasted & coarsely cracked in mortar","category":"Spices"},{"name":"Starchy pasta cooking water","amount":1,"unit":"cup","notes":"Boiled in minimal water for high starch","category":"Pantry"}]'::jsonb,
  '[{"step":1,"title":"Toast Black Peppercorns in Skillet","instruction":"Toast coarsely cracked black peppercorns in dry skillet over medium heat for 2 minutes until intensely fragrant. Add 0.5 cup starchy pasta water to create pepper broth.","time":"3 mins","temp":"Medium heat","visualCue":"Aromatic black pepper bubbling in cloudy water."},{"step":2,"title":"Make Pecorino Cheese Paste","instruction":"In a mixing bowl, gradually whisk 0.5 cup warm (not boiling) pasta water into the finely grated Pecorino until it forms a smooth, thick cheese paste.","time":"2 mins","temp":"Warm","visualCue":"Smooth, lump-free cheese paste."},{"step":3,"title":"Finish Pasta in Pepper Broth","instruction":"Boil pasta until 2 mins shy of al dente. Transfer directly into skillet with pepper broth. Toss vigorously over medium heat so pasta absorbs pepper flavor.","time":"2 mins","temp":"Medium heat","visualCue":"Noodles coated in glistening peppery reduction."},{"step":4,"title":"Off-Heat Cheese Emulsion","instruction":"Take pan completely off heat. Wait 30 seconds for pan to cool slightly (around 145°F). Add Pecorino paste and toss vigorously with tongs until sauce emulsifies into pure silk.","time":"2 mins","temp":"Off heat","visualCue":"Lustrous, velvety white cheese coat clinging to every strand."}]'::jsonb,
  '"Pecorino Romano clumps and turns into stringy chewing gum if added over boiling heat (above 150°F / 65°C); always let the pan cool slightly and toss off heat.","Cook pasta in half the usual amount of water so the water becomes ultra-concentrated in starch."',
