  '"Cast iron skillet or flat griddle","Heavy flat burger press or stiff metal spatula"',
  '[{"name":"Ground beef chuck (80/20 blend, loosely packed)","amount":350,"unit":"g","notes":"Formed into 4 chilled balls","category":"Proteins"},{"name":"Sharp American or mild cheddar cheese slices","amount":4,"unit":"slices","notes":"Fast melting","category":"Dairy"},{"name":"Brioche burger buns","amount":2,"unit":"buns","notes":"Buttered & toasted golden","category":"Pantry"},{"name":"Dill pickle chips & thinly sliced white onion","amount":0.5,"unit":"cup","notes":"Diner style","category":"Produce"},{"name":"Secret burger sauce (mayo, ketchup, relish, smoked paprika)","amount":3,"unit":"tbsp","notes":"Creamy tangy spread","category":"Pantry"}]'::jsonb,
  '[{"step":1,"title":"Heat Skillet to Blistering","instruction":"Place a dry cast iron skillet over high heat for 5 minutes until lightly smoking. Butter and toast brioche buns until golden.","time":"5 mins","temp":"High heat","visualCue":"Pan radiates intense dry heat; buns are crisp golden."},{"step":2,"title":"The High-Pressure Smash","instruction":"Place 2 chilled beef balls on hot skillet. Place parchment paper on top and smash with heavy press as flat as possible (1/4-inch). Season generously with salt and pepper.","time":"2 mins","temp":"High heat","visualCue":"Edges flatten into thin, lacy crinkles sizzled in fat."},{"step":3,"title":"Scrape, Flip & Melt Cheese","instruction":"After 2 minutes, scrape firmly with metal spatula under patties to retain all caramelized crust. Flip, immediately cover each with cheese, and stack two patties.","time":"1 min","temp":"High heat","visualCue":"Dark mahogany crust with molten cheese dripping down edges."},{"step":4,"title":"Assemble the Double Stack","instruction":"Spread secret sauce on toasted bottom bun. Lay double-stacked cheesy patties, top with sliced pickles and onions, and close bun.","time":"1 min","temp":"Hot","visualCue":"Juicy, molten double burger with crisp lace edges."}]'::jsonb,
  '"Use 80/20 ground chuck; leaner meat won't render enough fat to fry the lacy edges properly.","Keep the beef cold right until it hits the hot skillet; cold fat hitting screaming iron creates maximum Maillard browning."',
